---
// Lesson 5.1 — The Axe: Item Pickups & Inventory Bools
// hasAxe as boolean state; the pickup family grows up.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>5.1 — The Axe: Item Pickups &amp; Inventory Bools</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Coins and potions changed numbers. The axe changes what you
    <em>are</em>: it flips a boolean, swaps your animation variant, and
    suddenly you look like someone who can break down a door. Today:
    inventory as true/false flags — the cheapest data structure that
    can possibly work.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Predict First</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Look at the shipped Player prefab in Assets/Prefabs: it uses
        the <strong>PlayerAnimator with Axe/Shovel variants</strong> —
        the art for axe-wielding already exists. And in
        PlayerController, find the animation branch from
        <a href="1.6.html">1.6</a> that runs on mouse click
        (Attack trigger). The starter is aching to hand you an axe.
    </p>

    <p>
        Predict: where should "has an axe" live? A new int field? A
        list of items? A bool? Argue for your answer in one sentence
        before reading on — the starter's design uses a
        <strong>bool</strong> (hasAxe), because one item, one
        yes/no, is the honest data structure. Six items would be six
        bools; sixty would be a list (see the card jam in
        <a href="10.2a.html">10.2a</a> — that is the list era).
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            The AP exam loves questions that ask what data type fits
            the job. Why is a bool the right abstraction for "has an
            axe" but the WRONG one for "how many coins"? What does
            "right size the data" mean — one sentence.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — the field.</strong> In PlayerController, add
    the pickup family's newest member:</p>
    <pre><code>// Inventory
private bool hasAxe = false;</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — the pickup.</strong> The AxeItem prefab exists
    in Assets/Prefabs: check its collider (trigger) and its tag
    (Axe? add it if missing — same ritual as
    <a href="2.3.html">2.3</a>). Grow OnTriggerEnter2D with a third
    branch:</p>
    <pre><code>if (other.CompareTag("Axe"))
{
    hasAxe = true;
    // swap to the axe animation variant:
    // set the animator's parameter for the axe layer/variant here
    Destroy(other.gameObject);
}</code></pre>

    <p>
        Notice what this branch does NOT do: it touches no StatManager,
        prints no count. Not every pickup is a number. The axe is
        <em>capability</em>: a boolean that future code will read
        before allowing an action. You are building a permission.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — see the permission.</strong> Prove hasAxe is
    real state: add a temporary print in Update —</p>
    <pre><code>Debug.Log("hasAxe: " + hasAxe);</code></pre>
    <p>Run, watch it spam false, pick up the axe, watch it spam
    true. Delete the print after; the proof is in the behavior.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 4 — the art swap.</strong> Open the Player's
    Animator: the axe variant states exist but nothing transitions
    INTO them. Add an Animator bool parameter (axePickedUp, say), a
    transition from the normal set to the axe set on "true", and set
    it from your pickup branch — the same code-sets-parameter,
    animator-plays-frames split from <a href="1.6.html">1.6</a>,
    now driving an equipment change. When it works, your little
    sprite stops being a tourist.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: The phantom axe</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Set hasAxe = true as the field's starting value. What
                does the game grant you before you ever found it —
                and what did the default-after-pickup design protect
                against?</li>
            <li>Pick up the axe, die on a spike (death loop from
                <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a>), and watch your back.
                Do you still have the axe? Should you — and whose
                decision is that, code or designer?</li>
            <li>Add a SECOND axe to the map. Pick up both. What does
                hasAxe become after the second one — and why is
                "true" the only correct answer even though the count
                is two?</li>
            <li>You swap the animator parameter name to "hasAxe" in
                code but the Animator still says "axePickedUp".
                What is the symptom — silence, an error, or
                nothing?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Inventory design</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Add a shovel sibling (the art variant exists!). Two
                bools, two tags, two branches — at what number of
                items does copy-pasting branches become the wrong
                design?</li>
            <li>Design the HUD: how would the player SEE that they
                hold an axe? Argue sprite-only vs a UI icon — and
                which does the art variant already give you for
                free?</li>
            <li>Hard: one-use axe — breaking the door consumes it
                (hasAxe = false, swap back). Which script flips the
                bool back, and why must it NOT be the pickup branch?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Explain inventory bools vs counters and when each fits</li>
            <li>Build an item that grants capability instead of a number</li>
            <li>Drive an animation-variant swap from code through an Animator parameter</li>
            <li>Trace hasAxe across death (does it persist? should it?)</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-05/index.html">Unit 5: The Axe &amp; The Cave</a> — items, events, scenes</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/triggers-vs-collisions.html">Triggers vs Collisions</a> — the pickup family</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="5.2.html">5.2 — The Door: Breaking In with Events</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="5.1" title="5.1a The Axe: Item Pickups &amp; Inventory Bools — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
